GIT basics:


What Git ACTUALLY is (core idea)?

Git = a versioned database of snapshots
Git does not track “files changing”
Git tracks snapshots of your project at specific times
Each snapshot = commit




1. Git workflow stages: 
		"Working Directory---> Staging area ---> commit"

Working Directory
The file on disk (what you edit in VS Code)

Staging Area
Files you intend to save in the next snapshot

Commit (History)
A frozen snapshot saved permanently




2. git status (MOST IMPORTANT command)
	git status

This tells you:

Which branch you are on
What files are changed
What is staged
What is untracked
Always run this when confused.



3. git add .      means “Stage ALL modified files”

It moves the file:
Working directory → Staging area


4. git commit -m "message"    — THE SNAPSHOT

Takes everything in the staging area
Saves a snapshot
Adds it to the history

After commit:
Working directory → clean
History → one more commit


5.Remote branches vs local branches

| Local (Laptop) | Remote (GitHub)     |
| -------------- | ------------------- |
| `main`         | `origin/main`       |
| `experiment`   | `origin/experiment` |


6.git fetch vs git pull

git fetch  --> “Download info from GitHub, but don’t touch my files”  It updates origin/main , but not main. Safe, read only.
git pull   --> “Download + merge into my current branch”  So git pull changes your code.
	Internally git pull does:
	git fetch
	git merge origin/main






CHEAT SHEET:

1.Start work:
git switch main
git pull

2.New IDEA (create a new branch) or (switch to a existing branch):
| Situation             | Command                 |
| --------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Branch does NOT exist | `git switch -c my-idea` |
| Branch already exists | `git switch my-idea`    |

git switch -c my-idea
git switch my-idea


3.Save progress by pushing the locally created branch into the github 
git add .
git commit -m "message"
git push -u origin my-idea

4.Merge to main: (later if you want to merge the branch to main if you think there wont be conflicts)
git switch main
git pull
git merge my-idea
git push



IMP NOTE:
👉 Creating a branch locally does NOT create it on GitHub

After:
"git switch -c my-idea"

This branch exists:
✔ locally
❌ NOT on GitHub

To create it on GitHub, you must push it:
"git push -u origin my-idea"

the above command creates the branch on GitHub and links it to your local branch.
in future , you can just do git push to push changes to that branch on GitHub.
no need to do "git push -u origin my-idea again."
